lklfuse: on Darwin, -o volicon=PATH shows PATH as the volume's icon while the volume has none of its own

A Mac volume's icon is its root's .VolumeIcon.icns, shown when the root's Finder Info has kHasCustomIcon. A Linux
volume has neither, so Finder showed macFUSE's generic icon. linuxfs makes its own at install (the Mac's
External.icns with Tux, in each bundle's Resources: spec item 6's amendment) and passes it as volicon=. While the
volume root has no /.VolumeIcon.icns, lookup and getattr of that name answer a regular file (0644, the root's owner
and group, so mapped as every other file; PATH's size and times; an inode number past every filesystem's), an open
for reading and read serve PATH's bytes (an fh that is never an LKL descriptor, so read, release and getattr know it
without a path: nullpath_ok), and getxattr com.apple.FinderInfo on / is the volume's own (patch 0013 stores Mac
xattrs), or a 32-byte FolderInfo of zeros when it has none, with kHasCustomIcon set (0x0400 in frFlags, bytes 8-9
big-endian), which listxattr of / then names, once. readdir does not list it,
and lklfuse itself writes nothing to the disk. The icon's bytes and attributes cannot be changed, nor can it be
moved or linked: write or ftruncate through its fh, chmod, chown, utimens, setxattr, removexattr, rename away and a
hard link to it get EACCES. Unlinking it gives ENOENT, as nothing of that name is on the disk, so unlink-then-create
replacers (install, ln -sf, cp -f) go on. The user may put their own icon there, as on HFS+: making a file under the
name (create; an open with O_TRUNC, which is cp's, since the kernel sees a file there; truncate; rename or link onto
it; mkdir, mknod, symlink) acts on the disk as if the name were free, so a real /.VolumeIcon.icns is made, owned as
any new file. An open for writing without O_TRUNC (an in-place edit, or a lock) makes it a copy of the icon's bytes,
with O_EXCL, so a file someone else made meanwhile is opened as it is; a copy that fails is undone. A real one wins: it is looked for on every call (lookups are not
cached), so one made or removed shows at once, and the root's FinderInfo is then the volume's own. Setting the
root's FinderInfo stores the volume's own, and the flag is never written. PATH is opened once, at start; when it cannot be
read, that is said and the volume shows macFUSE's icon. The code is portable; only Darwin takes the option.
tests/lklfuse-ops-test.c checks each of these, including the real file winning, cp, mv and truncate onto the icon,
an in-place patch, unlink-then-create, and readdir.

